course: dictionary
lesson: 14061
title: Big words for chewing
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Masticate is a big, formal word for chew. When you chew your food into small pieces before you swallow it, you masticate it.
explain: «Remember to masticate your food well before you swallow.» The word swallow comes after masticate, so chewing happens first. Masticate means to chew food into small pieces before swallowing.
explain: In science, your teeth do this job. Molars grind the food, incisors cut it, and saliva softens it. Scientists call this mastication, and it is the first step of digestion.
fact: Masticate means to chew food so it breaks into small pieces before swallowing.
fact: Masticate is a verb: masticate, masticates, masticated, masticating.
fact: The noun for this action is mastication, as in «mastication by the teeth».
fact: In digestion, mastication is the first step: molars grind, incisors cut, and saliva softens the food.
fact: In technical work, to masticate rubber means to soften it by crushing and kneading it.
exercise: «Remember to masticate your food well before you swallow.» What does masticate mean? | Swallow the food whole | *Chew food into small pieces | Cook food in hot water | Wash your hands before eating
exercise: In science, what do molars do during mastication? | Swallow the food | Freeze the food | Cut the food into thin strips | *Grind the food
exercise: Which word is the noun form of masticate? | Masterpiece | *Mastication | Mastered | Massacre
test: «Saliva softens the food as you masticate it.» What does saliva do to the food? | Makes it harder | *Softens it | Turns it cold | Cuts it into pieces
test: In digestion, what is the first step? | Stomach acid | *Mastication by the teeth | Drinking water | Swallowing
exam: Mia crushes and kneads the rubber so it will be soft. She is trying to masticate it. What is she doing to the rubber? | Freezing it | *Softening it | Painting it | Cutting it into strips
